<</BS<</W 0>>/CA 1/Contents(Logical Persuasion - She doesn't have that "knack" for interactions or tasks that could require someone's "charisma", therefore her more objective way of thinking allows her to use tools such as abstraction and analogies to portray logic and facts in a fa\
vorable manner. Outside of that, though, there's hardly more that she could do than use your common Occam's Razor, for example.)/DA(0 0 0 RG 0 0 0 rg /Helv 9 Tf)/DS(font: Helvetica,sans-serif 9.0pt; text-align:left; color:#000000 )/F 4/M(D:20240918140555-03'00')/NM(4ad9b17ed327424288eb2d55c9c580e4)/Page 2/RC(<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:24.3.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"  style="font-size:9.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal\
;font-family:Helvetica,sans-serif;font-stretch:normal"><p dir="ltr"><span style="font-family:Helvetica">Logical Persuasion - She doesn't have that &quot;knack&quot; for interactions or tasks that could require someone's &quot;charisma&quot;, therefore her\
 more objective way of thinking allows her to use tools such as abstraction and analogies to portray logic and facts in a favorable manner. Outside of that, though, there's hardly more that she could do than use your common Occam's Razor, for example.</sp\
an></p></body>)/Rect[180.873 115.427 515.13 173.437]/Subj(Text Box)/Subtype/FreeText/T( )/Type/Annot>>
endobj
